---
/*
  The scroll indicator under a plate strip on a phone.

  WHY IT EXISTS

  A row of three or more plates becomes a sideways strip at phone widths, and
  the scrollbar is hidden there on purpose - a native bar is noise across a
  shelf of pictures, and on iOS it is an overlay that only appears mid-gesture
  anyway, so at rest there was nothing at all saying the row moved. The cut
  edge of the next plate is a hint; it is not a control, and it says nothing
  about how much more there is.

  This says both: how much of the row is on screen (the thumb's length) and
  where you are in it (its position).

  IN THE SITE'S OWN VOCABULARY

  Square, hairline track, solid thumb, no radius - the same construction as
  the archive switch's track and thumb a few hundred lines up in global.css,
  which is the only other place this site draws a control as a bar with a
  block on it.

  currentColor rather than a fixed grey, because a case study band takes the
  project's accent colour and a hardcoded rule would be invisible on a dark
  accent and heavy on a light one. Same reasoning the section hairlines used
  before they were removed.

  aria-hidden: it reports the state of a scroll container the reader can
  already scroll, and announcing "0.4" to a screen reader is noise. The strip
  itself is a focusable scroll container, which is what keyboard and assistive
  navigation actually need.

  Inert until the script measures something to scroll - see Sections.astro. A
  row whose plates happen to fit shows nothing.
*/
---

<div class="strip-rule" aria-hidden="true">
  <span class="strip-rule__thumb"></span>
</div>
